    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d313">

                <pb facs="0314" n="306" sameAs="frus:frus1939v05/pg306"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">824.00/908</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of the American
                        Republics</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Duggan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Legal Adviser</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Hackworth</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-05-11">May 11, 1939</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Hackworth</hi>: With further reference to our
                    exchange of memoranda concerning recognition and at the risk of imposing a
                    hypothetical question, may I inquire your opinion as to whether an act or a
                    series of acts as set forth in the last page of your memorandum of May 3 would
                    be necessary in case a president were not only to dismiss the congress and annul
                    the constitution, but to set up an entirely new regime upon totally different
                    concepts.</p>
                <pb facs="0315" n="307" xml:id="pg307"/>
                <p>In other words, what I am striving to find out is whether in case of a change in
                    regime which, while not changing the president, resulted in a switch let us say
                    from a democratic government to a totalitarian government, would recognition be
                    necessary or unnecessary? Is there a line that can be drawn between changes in
                    government which appear to be a continuation of the previous government and a
                    change of government in which, while the president may remain the same, the
                    whole bases of the political, economic and social structure of the country are
                    altered?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Laurence Duggan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
